WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  a show of descent in the heart of  political decision-making in baghdad  [Applause]  protesters taking over the speaker's  plinth and mp's benches  their supporters of the influential shia  cleric  he says he does not want muhammad  al-sudani to be the next prime minister  and his supporters are likely attempting  to enforce this  this is only from baghdad he didn't  bring his supporters from other  provinces  stormed the green zone the parliament  they're in good numbers it seems to me  that there is a plan of escalating this  protesting movement so to speak  he basically wants to dislodge the  the candidate for the premiership  and this is how they got through the  heavily fortified so-called green zone  undeterred by water cannon protesters  ripped through the cement blast walls  surrounding a 10 kilometer stretch of  the city  dozens pass with apparent ease forcing  their way down the main thoroughfare  towards parliament  they remained there until sada  instructed them to withdraw  in a tweet he described it as a  revolution of reform and rejection of  corruption saying the message had been  delivered and the protesters have  terrified the corrupt in power  sadda is one of the most popular  politicians in iraq his party was the  biggest winner in october's election but  he failed to form a majority coalition  last month more than 70 mps allied with  the shia cleric resigned from parliament  calling it corrupt  now the pro-iran coordination framework  is nominating sudani as prime minister  for now this is a victory for sardis and  a threat to political decision makers  analysts say protesters are likely to  maintain the pressure from the streets  law bowden manly al jazeera

